<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Felipe Santiago Salaverry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1806">1806</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Felipe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> Salaverry was a Peruvian general who seized power in 1835 and ruled as Supreme Chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. He resisted the intervention of the Bolivian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a>, who had come to the aid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-jose-de-orbegoso">Luis José de Orbegoso</a>. Defeated at the Battle of Socabaya in February 1836, he was captured and shot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a>, clearing the way for the Peru–Bolivian Confederation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felipe-santiago-salaverry.jpg" alt="Felipe Santiago Salaverry" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salaverry2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
